WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.743
- This video was sponsored by Squarespace.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.200
Almost every single
self-improvement YouTuber,

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.810
self-improvement book,
motivational speaker will tell you

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.270
how important it is to have confidence,

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:16.750
confidence in yourself,

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.140
confidence in everything that you do.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.160
They'll also go on and on and on

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:24.370
about how important it is
to have high self-esteem,

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:26.320
and to feel sure about yourself

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.030
and to know that no matter
what you set your mind to,

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:30.280
you're capable of doing it.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.890
Never accept less than what
you think you're worth.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:35.170
Never stay in a job that you don't like.

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:37.920
Don't keep doing things
that you don't truly love.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.940
And as a society, we just
sort of accept this advice

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.320
as if it's gospel, as
if there is no downside

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.110
to this radical self-assurance,
but like any philosophy,

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.720
I think it's unwise to not question it.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:51.940
And I think that this sort of

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.650
blind optimism is worth questioning.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:56.300
In fact, I think it's the very reason

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.900
why so many people feel so stuck in life,

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.830
because one of the main downsides

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:03.280
to this unconditional self-confidence

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.430
in everything that you do,
is that it can quickly turn

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:09.290
into an inflated sense
of what we're capable of,

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.480
which blinds us from
correcting our weaknesses

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.960
changing paths if something's not working.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.740
And that can really prevent
us from improving our lives

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.500
or making progress in any substantial way,

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.800
because there's a word
for an inflated sense

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.830
of self-confidence, one that's
beyond the realm of reality.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:29.440
We call that ego.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.570
You know, when your sense
of your own capabilities

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:36.410
and importance in life is
bigger than reality itself,

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:37.960
it's called having a big ego

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.890
and sometimes having a big ego
is really difficult to spot.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:44.150
I know for a fact it was in my own life.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.150
I've talked a lot on this
channel about how when I went

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:49.900
to university, it wasn't a
filmmaking specific university.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:53.710
So in my intro level film
classes, I was the best filmmaker

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.270
in the room, or so I thought,
but as soon as somebody

00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:59.130
in the class had a film that
maybe they put more effort

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.750
into than I did, I felt
threatened by this,

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:02.970
and I was in denial.

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.860
I started looking for faults in their work

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.230
and there's no way that they
know how to work a camera

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.330
or have better cinematography than I do.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.800
And that blinded myself from
my own weaknesses in my work.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.100
You know, I was living in this sort

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.886
of fantasy land where
I'm this master filmmaker

00:02:16.886 --> 00:02:19.840
even though I'm not even
close to being that.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.230
And I knew I wasn't,

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:23.920
but my ego was blinding
me from the reality.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.541
It was blinding me from my weaknesses

00:02:25.541 --> 00:02:27.840
and things that I needed to work on

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.840
which prevented me from reaching the goal

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.337
of actually becoming
a masterful filmmaker,

00:02:32.337 --> 00:02:34.900
a goal which I'm nowhere
near accomplishing.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.700
Your ego is kind of addicting.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.560
It tells you all the nice
things that you want to hear.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.430
It tells you how good and
special and talented you are,

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.190
but it doesn't really help you improve.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:46.760
You know, your ego is
different from confidence

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.680
because confidence stems from competence.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.510
As you get better at things
you develop a track record

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:54.620
of being competent at that thing.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:56.410
So that you know for a fact

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:58.320
that you can pull this off,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.930
you're capable of doing
this, that's confidence.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.760
You gain confidence through
repetition and mastery.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.690
Your ego tells you that you're talented

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:08.360
and special and you can
do whatever you wanna do,

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:11.490
but it's not actually grounded
in a track record of success.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.350
I think one of the most common examples

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:15.220
of people's ego getting in the way

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.880
of their success is when
they're working a job

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.810
that they feel like they're better than,

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.550
maybe it's an entry level position.

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:24.260
Maybe they're working at
a fast food restaurant.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:25.510
They're flipping burgers,

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:27.510
and they have an undergraduate degree,

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.320
and they're super educated in some field

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.810
yet they still find themselves
stuck in entry-level jobs,

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.440
and they start thinking to
themselves, what am I doing here?

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:37.400
I'm better than this.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.600
I'm more educated than all of my peers.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:40.820
I deserve better.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.180
A lot of the time, that's
just your ego talking.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.510
It's blinding you from the
reality that perhaps your resume

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:48.810
isn't that impressive to the employers

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.510
in the field that you want to go into.

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:51.930
Perhaps you should accept the loss

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:55.260
of time in whatever degree you got,

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.460
or whatever jobs you were working before.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:00.340
Take a slice of humble pie and figure out

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:03.270
the best way forward from
an objective perspective,

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.970
no matter how unflattering
that path might look

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.000
on the surface level.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.300
Our ego blinds us from reality.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.670
And if we can't accept
reality we can't improve it.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:15.460
In the book Ego Is The
Enemy by Ryan Holiday.

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:17.530
He talks about how you can aspire to be

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:19.700
either one of two things.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:21.600
You can aspire to be somebody,

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.460
or you can aspire to do something.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.370
And he argues that we live
in a world filled with people

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:27.850
trying to be somebody.

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:30.000
In fact, you're kind of
told that your whole life,

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.430
go out there and be somebody.

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:34.452
So naturally people want
to get their lucky break.

00:04:34.452 --> 00:04:37.000
They wanna be recognized as special.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:38.240
They want to work their way up

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.450
in the company so that
they can eventually sit

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:42.380
at the big desk with the big boys.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.590
If you're an artist you
really wanna be recognized

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:45.920
for your talent, you know

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.030
get your lucky break, become famous.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:51.090
You know, and everybody wants
to be an Instagram influencer,

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.350
or a TikTok star or a YouTuber.

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:54.690
They wanna have millions of people

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:56.610
recognizing how special they are.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:57.630
They wanna be somebody.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.490
They wanna have influence,
that's the more tempting option.

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.180
The other option is to
aspire to do something,

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.440
to get something done, to have a purpose

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.640
or a mission doing the best
you can do in your craft.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.060
This type of person is a lot
more concerned about action

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.530
and education rather than
validation and status.

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:17.500
The person who aspires to do something

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:20.590
in life is a lot more
concerned with being useful

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:23.600
rather than appearing to
be useful or looking busy.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.280
This is especially relevant
for artists and creatives.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.160
If you're an influencer or a YouTuber

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.070
I think you really need
to hear this as well.

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:34.130
If your video, if your art, if your craft,

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.250
whatever it is, is good enough.

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:37.630
It's its own marketing.

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:38.960
You know, if you double down

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.706
and put in 10 times more
effort into your next video,

00:05:41.706 --> 00:05:45.430
if you put in 10 times more
effort into your next art piece

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:47.990
then the work will kind
of speak for itself.

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:49.210
You know, there's a higher chance

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:51.600
that it will become
extremely useful to people.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.100
And then the word of mouth
effect will start to take place.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.650
You know, every single time
that I start to get caught up

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:58.470
with how special I am for having

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:00.130
almost a million subscribers,

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:01.820
and how am a big YouTuber

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:03.910
and all my videos get lots of views.

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:05.850
As soon as I start thinking that way,

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.930
I start focusing on the wrong things.

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:11.070
My videos start to suffer,
you know, the actual quality

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.260
of the videos and the message
starts to suffer dramatically.

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:15.940
The best videos I've ever made.

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.670
The ones I'm super proud of are videos

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:20.720
where I was just really concerned

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.100
with telling the best story
or getting my message across

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.850
in the best way possible,

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:27.510
in the most effective way possible.

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:31.070
I treated the video in and
of itself as the end goal

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:33.000
and whether or not that video blows up

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.040
in the algorithm is kind of irrelevant,

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:36.950
because I became that much better

00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:38.760
as a filmmaker and a storyteller.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:41.510
And my existing audience
takes notice of that,

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:42.970
and they really appreciate it.

00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:45.370
And when you offer that
much value to people

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:47.040
and you're that useful to people

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:48.990
everything sort of comes into alignment,

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:50.600
the views and the subscribers

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.130
and the comments and the likes.

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:54.180
They all kind of take care of themselves.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:55.920
So I guess what I'm trying
to get at with this video

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:57.680
is no matter what you're into,

00:06:57.680 --> 00:06:59.420
no matter what your profession

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:01.340
or your hobby or your craft is

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:03.670
you need to take on the role of a student.

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:07.270
Every single person wants
to be noticed and respected.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.960
But I think the path
forward to dramatic progress

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.950
in your life is the
willingness to become a nobody

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.760
until your results put you
in a different category.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.460
You know, if you're applying for a job,

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:19.820
how much better would it be if your resume

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:22.840
was three times better than anyone else's,

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:24.880
not from a formatting perspective,

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:27.925
not from you using fluffy
language to describe yourself,

00:07:27.925 --> 00:07:31.260
but by you being three
times more qualified,

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:34.630
experienced, educated than anybody else

00:07:34.630 --> 00:07:36.100
applying for that same job.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:38.230
Stop caring so much
about what other people

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:41.230
think about you, how
you appear in the world,

00:07:41.230 --> 00:07:43.730
and actually be someone
who is useful to people,

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:46.490
so useful that not having you in the room

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:49.750
or part of the team, or the
company is such an immediate

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:52.245
and dramatic detriment
to everyone around you,

00:07:52.245 --> 00:07:55.230
that they have no choice but
to always have you around,

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:56.670
because you're that useful.

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:59.090
I guess if I was to distill
this entire video down

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:02.020
into just a couple of
sentences, a little recap,

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:04.440
it would be that no matter
where you are in life,

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:06.710
if you've experienced a lot of success,

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:08.630
and you're still unfulfilled,

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:11.160
and you feel like you're
kind of a house of cards

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:12.260
awaiting to fall down,

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:14.560
or maybe you haven't
experienced a lot of success

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.393
in your life at all, and you feel stuck,

00:08:16.393 --> 00:08:18.330
pay attention to your ego,

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:20.740
pay attention to what this
is little sinister voice

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:22.600
in the back of your head is telling you,

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:24.570
is telling you you're too good for this.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.800
You're already the best
and you can improve,

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:28.410
because that's all kind of bullshit.

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:29.800
Strip away the ego,

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:32.100
try to view your position with a ruthless,

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:33.910
objectivity and honesty,

00:08:33.910 --> 00:08:36.440
and doing that will reveal a path forward.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.400
And no matter how painful
that path forward may seem,

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:42.250
no matter how embarrassing it might look,

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:43.750
see what happens if you take it,

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:46.453
because going down that unflattering path

00:08:46.453 --> 00:08:49.363
maybe the answer to your problems.

